  Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["7. Variational derivative of top-degree forms", 
      "Section", CellID -> 1100360], 
     Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["FormVarD\t\t ", "Input", FontWeight -> "Plain", 
         FontColor -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], StyleBox[
         "Variational derivative of a form with rank equal to the dimension."\
, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0]]}], "DefinitionBox", CellID -> 1100361], 
     Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["FormVarD", FontFamily -> "Source Code Pro"], 
        " is similar to the ", StyleBox["xTensor", FontFamily -> 
          "Source Code Pro"], " command ", StyleBox["VarD", 
         FontFamily -> "Source Code Pro"], ", but for top-rank forms. A \
derivative is not specified, because the exterior derivative is natural. \
Within this approach, the variation of the integral of a top-rank form also \
involves the adjoint to the exterior derivative. However, the adjoint to the \
exterior derivative, the codifferential, requires a Hodge dual, which it is \
defined via a (non-degenerate!) metric. Therefore, in addition to a form, ", 
        StyleBox["FormVarD", FontFamily -> "Source Code Pro"], 
        " requires also a metric as an argument. "}], "Text", 
      CellID -> 1100362], Cell["Example: Electromagnetism", "Subsection", 
      CellID -> 1100363], Cell["As the first example we take the Maxwell \
